  "question": "A previously healthy 31-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of sudden, severe epigastric pain and vomiting for the past 4 hours. She reports that the pain radiates to the back and began when she was having dinner and drinks at a local brewpub. Her temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), pulse is 98/min, respirations are 19/min, and blood pressure is 110/60 mm Hg. Abdominal examination shows epigastric tenderness and guarding but no rebound. Bowel sounds are decreased. Laboratory studies show:\nHematocrit 43%\nLeukocyte count 9000/mm3\nSerum\nNa+ 140 mEq/L\nK+ 4.5 mEq/L\nCa2+ 9.0 mg/dL\nLipase 170 U/L (N = < 50 U/L)\nAmylase 152 U/L\nAlanine aminotransferase (ALT, GPT) 140 U/L\nIntravenous fluid resuscitation is begun. Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?\"?",
